{ id:'enthd-nose-290', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'**After controlling an episode of epistaxis**, which of the following is the **next step** in management?',
  options:['Cautery','Nasal packing','Arterial clipping','All of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Cautery — once the bleeding has stopped the bleeding point can finally be SEEN, and sealing it is what stops the next attack.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` splits the whole subject on one slide — *"Bleeding — **Active** / **Inactive**"* — and everything after it follows that split. The three options offered here are not alternatives at the same moment; they are three different moments.\n\n**The deck orders anterior epistaxis management as a sequence:** *"Clear the clots → Cotton soaked with decongestant → **Cautery** → Ant. Nasal pack"*. Note where the pack sits: **after** cautery, not before it. And the deck states plainly when a pack is chosen instead: *"Anterior Nasal Pack… **Cautery is not available. Cautery fails. Bleeding site is not identified.**"* A pack is the fallback, not the follow-up.\n\n**Why cautery is the step that belongs after control.** Chemical cautery needs a dry, visible field: *"Silver nitrate sticks can be applied over the Little’s Area… **Light application over the area of origin of bleeding** may result in cauterisation of the underlying vessels… lightly wiping the tip of the silver nitrate stick over the Little’s area **until it becomes discoloured to a grey-white**."* You cannot wipe a stick over a spot that is under a jet of blood, which is exactly why this is a post-control manoeuvre. The deck also lists the alternatives — *"Chemical, Electric, Laser (Nd-YAG), cryotherapy"*.\n\n| Option | The moment it belongs to | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cautery** | after control, when the vessel in Little’s area is visible and dry — definitive treatment of the **source** | **keyed** |\n| **Nasal packing** | **during** active bleeding, and only when cautery is unavailable, has failed, or the site cannot be found; it works *"by pressure and creation of mucosal oedema"*, treats no vessel, and its own complication list includes *"Induce rebleeding"* | wrong moment |\n| **Arterial clipping** | far later still — the deck reserves ligation/embolization for *"Continued bleeding despite nasal packing"* (`enthd-nose-292`, `enthd-nose-315`) | wrong moment |\n| **All of the above** | collapses a deliberate sequence into a list | wrong |\n\n**Bottom line: control the bleeding, then treat the bleeding point.** Anterior epistaxis in a child or young adult comes from Little’s area — *"the usual site for epistaxis in children and young adults"* — and cautery of that spot is what converts a recurrent problem into a treated one. Grade Gain keys chemical cautery among the accepted treatments of primary childhood epistaxis (`entqb-nose2-69`, and see `enthd-nose-314`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Once epistaxis is controlled and the bleeding point is visible and dry, cautery of Little’s area is the next step; packing is the alternative used during active bleeding when cautery is unavailable or fails, and arterial ligation only after packing fails.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.86 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). The active/inactive split, the anterior-epistaxis sequence *"clear the clots — cotton soaked with decongestant — cautery — ant. nasal pack"*, the three indications for packing, the silver-nitrate technique and the four cautery modalities are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. Chemical cautery as an accepted treatment of primary childhood epistaxis is Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose2-69`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-291', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **best material** to be used in **stoppage of active bleeding**?',
  options:['Vaseline-soaked gauze','Merocel tampon','Biodegradable nasal packing','All of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Biodegradable packing — it is the one material that never has to be pulled back out, and removal is when a nasal pack undoes its own work.**\n\nAll three named materials stop bleeding, and `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` teaches all three. What separates them is what happens afterwards. The deck gives an anterior pack a complication list, and two of the five entries are about removal or the trauma of the pack itself:\n\n> *"Anterior Nasal Pack — Complications: **Uncomfortable**. **Vasovagal attack (during insertion & removal)**. Secondary sinusitis. Toxic shock syndrome. **Induce rebleeding**."*\n\nA traditional petrolatum-gauze pack is a long strip laid *"in an accordion fashion"* against raw, freshly cauterised or ulcerated mucosa; when it is withdrawn it takes the clot with it, and the patient bleeds again. Merocel is a compressed sponge that expands on wetting — the same pressure principle, still a foreign body, still removed. The deck files **biodegradable** material separately and forwards, on its own slide: *"Anterior Nasal Pack — **Recent Modifications** … **Biodegradable**"*.\n\n| Option | How it works | Why it is not *best* |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Vaseline-soaked gauze** | the traditional layered strip; the deck details the technique — *"Grasp the petrolatum gauze with bayonet forceps about 10 cm from the end… parallel to the nasal floor"* | must be removed; the deck’s own list includes *"Induce rebleeding"* and vasovagal attack on removal |\n| **Merocel tampon** | expanding compressed sponge, quicker and better tolerated than gauze | still a removable foreign body with the same removal problem |\n| **Biodegradable nasal packing** | haemostatic material that is **absorbed in situ** — no removal, therefore no removal-rebleed and no second vasovagal episode | **keyed** |\n| **All of the above** | the stem asks for the **best**, not for a list of what works | wrong by the wording of the question |\n\n**⚠️ Note the pairing with `entep-nose-14`, which asks the opposite question on almost the same menu.** There, Merocel, vaseline gauze and nasal balloons are all acceptable in a cardiac patient and **adrenaline-soaked cotton** is the exception. Here every listed material is acceptable and the question is one of rank. The two items together are the deck’s point: choose the material for the patient in front of you.\n\n**Bottom line: pressure stops the bleed, but the pack you never have to remove is the one that keeps it stopped.** In a patient on anticoagulants or with a coagulopathy — one of the deck’s systemic factors, *"Coagulopathies (hemophilia, anticoagulants)"* — that advantage is decisive.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Biodegradable nasal packing is preferred for active anterior bleeding because it is absorbed in place, avoiding the rebleeding and vasovagal attack that follow removal of gauze or Merocel packs.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.86 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). The anterior-pack complication list, the petrolatum-gauze technique, the *"Recent Modifications — Biodegradable"* slide and *"Coagulopathies (hemophilia, anticoagulants)"* are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. **The reason biodegradable material is ranked best — absorption in situ removing the removal-rebleed — is stated by no cached deck as such and is inferred directly from the deck’s own complication list.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-292', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'All of the following are among **indications of surgical intervention** in case of epistaxis **except**?',
  options:['Uncontrolled nosebleed despite nasal packing','Nasal congenital anomalies','Intolerant patient','Need for blood transfusion','None of the above'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**None of the above — all four are indications, because the question has simply printed the lecture’s own list back in different words.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` gives four indications on a single slide, and each of the four options is one of them:\n\n> *"**Indications for surgery/embolization**: Continued bleeding despite nasal packing. Nasal anomaly precluding packing. Patient refusal/intolerance of packing. Pt requires transfusion/admit hct of <38%."*\n\n| Option | The deck’s own wording | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Uncontrolled nosebleed despite nasal packing** | *"Continued bleeding despite nasal packing"* | an indication |\n| **Nasal congenital anomalies** | *"Nasal anomaly precluding packing"* — a nose that cannot be packed cannot be treated by packing | an indication |\n| **Intolerant patient** | *"Patient refusal/intolerance of packing"* | an indication |\n| **Need for blood transfusion** | *"Pt requires transfusion/admit hct of <38%"* | an indication |\n| **None of the above** | there is no exception on this list | **keyed** |\n\n**The logic behind the list is worth more than the list.** Three of the four are not about how bad the bleeding is at all — they are about **packing having become impossible or unacceptable**. Two ways to fail a pack are anatomical (an anomaly that will not hold one) and behavioural (a patient who will not tolerate one), and the deck weighs both as heavily as continued bleeding. The fourth, the transfusion threshold, converts a nosebleed into a systemic problem: once blood has to be replaced, the risk of continuing to wait exceeds the risk of operating.\n\n**What the surgery actually is.** The deck lists the open options — *"Internal maxillary artery. Anterior +/- posterior ethmoid ligation. External carotid ligation"* — and then dates them: in its 2023 slide, **IMAX ligation** and **ethmoid ligation** remain, **SPA coagulation** and **ethmoid coagulation** are the endoscopic equivalents, and **external carotid ligation** is marked *"Obsolete"*. Its outcome table settles why: packing succeeds 68 % with 35 % complications and 4.2 days in hospital, transantral ligation/percutaneous embolization 88 % and 27.5 %, and endoscopic control *"> 90 %"* with *"No major"* complications and *"< one day"* stay — hence the deck’s *"SUGGESTED CHANGE: **Initial definitive endoscopic control** +/- temporary packing"*.\n\n**Bottom line: an indication for surgery in epistaxis is either bleeding that packing has not stopped, or a patient in whom packing is not an option — and the modern answer to both is endoscopic, not open.** ⚠️ **Five options are printed, A–E.** See `enthd-nose-315` for the artery chosen when combined packing fails.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The four indications for surgery or embolization in epistaxis are continued bleeding despite packing, a nasal anomaly precluding packing, patient intolerance or refusal of packing, and the need for transfusion — so on this menu there is no exception.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.86 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). The four indications, the ligation options, the 2023 open-versus-endoscopic slide with *"External carotid ligation — Obsolete"*, the 68 %/88 %/>90 % outcome table and *"Initial definitive endoscopic control"* are all transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. **⚠️ Five options, A–E; the key is E.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-293', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'A **32-year-old female** presents with nosebleed. **You could not detect the bleeding spot**, so you performed **anterior nasal packing — but it did not stop the bleeding and she continued to spit blood out of her mouth**. What is your provisional diagnosis?',
  options:['Resistant anterior epistaxis','Mild anterior epistaxis','Posterior epistaxis','Angiofibroma'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Posterior epistaxis — because the lecture defines it by exactly these two failures, and by nothing else.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` gives a definition that is entirely clinical, not anatomical:\n\n> *"Posterior epistaxis is bleeding that arises **so far posterior that the bleeding site can not be identified by anterior inspection** or **controlled by anterior pack**."*\n\nThe vignette prints both halves. The spot could not be seen on anterior inspection; the anterior pack did not control it. That is the definition satisfied twice over, and no further information is needed.\n\n**The blood in the mouth is the third sign and the most visible one.** A bleeding point behind an anterior pack has only one way out — backwards through the choana, down the nasopharynx and into the oropharynx, so the patient swallows or spits blood. Anteriorly the nose may look dry and controlled while the patient is still losing blood. The deck names the vessel: posterior epistaxis *"comes most commonly from the **SPA** or one of its branches"*, and the plexus involved is **Woodruff’s** — *"a vascular network located on the lateral wall **posterior to the inferior turbinate**… generally considered to be responsible for posterior epistaxis"*, an *"anastomosis between the ascending pharyngeal and post. nasal arteries of the sphenopalatine artery"* (`entqb-nose2-71`).\n\n| Option | Why it is wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **Resistant anterior epistaxis** | the trap, and the reason the stem stresses *couldn’t detect the bleeding spot*. Anterior epistaxis is *"usually septal, from Kiesselbach’s plexus"*, i.e. **visible on anterior rhinoscopy**. A visible spot that resists treatment is still anterior; an **invisible** one is posterior by definition |\n| **Mild anterior epistaxis** | the deck calls anterior bleeding *"typically less severe"*, but nothing here is mild — packing has failed and she is spitting blood |\n| **Posterior epistaxis** | **keyed** |\n| **Angiofibroma** | wrong patient entirely. Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma is a tumour of **adolescent males**; the corpus keys it repeatedly on that demographic (`entqb-nose2-59`, `entep-nose-82`, `entqb-nose7-228`). A 32-year-old woman with a first nosebleed does not have one |\n\n**Bottom line: posterior versus anterior is decided by what you can see and what a pack can reach, not by how much blood there is.** Epidemiology supports the reading — the deck’s bimodal curve puts posterior bleeding in *"elderly hypertensive patients"* and anterior bleeding at the childhood peak — but this patient is diagnosed on the two failed manoeuvres, not on her age. Management follows at `enthd-nose-294` (posterior pack) and `enthd-nose-315` (when the combined pack also fails).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Posterior epistaxis is defined as bleeding whose site cannot be identified by anterior inspection and cannot be controlled by an anterior pack — typically from the sphenopalatine artery and Woodruff’s plexus, and betrayed by blood running into the pharynx.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.87 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). The definition of posterior epistaxis, the anterior/posterior contrast, the sphenopalatine source and Woodruff’s plexus are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`; Woodruff’s plexus as the origin of posterior epistaxis is also Grade Gain’s printed key in `entqb-nose2-71`. **The demographic of juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma used to exclude option D is the corpus’s own repeated key (`entqb-nose2-59`, `entep-nose-82`).**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-294', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'**Posterior nasal packing is mandatory** in which of the following situations?',
  options:['Severe epistaxis in a shocked patient','Failure of bleeding cessation by anterior nasal packing','Inability to localize site of bleeding','All of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Failure of an anterior pack — that failure IS the definition of posterior epistaxis, and a posterior pack is the only thing on the list that reaches further back than an anterior one.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` defines the disease by the failed manoeuvre: *"Posterior epistaxis is bleeding that arises so far posterior that the bleeding site can not be identified by anterior inspection or **controlled by anterior pack**."* Its management slide then begins where the anterior pack stopped — *"Posterior epistaxis - management: **Deep ant. Pack → Ant-Post Pack →** IMAX ligation / Embolization, Ethmoid ligation, ECA ligation."*\n\n**⚠️ Option C is the trap, and the deck kills it explicitly.** *"Bleeding site is not identified"* is printed by the lecture as one of the three indications for an **ANTERIOR** pack, not a posterior one:\n\n> *"Anterior Nasal Pack… **Cautery is not available. Cautery fails. Bleeding site is not identified.**"*\n\nAn anterior pack works *"by pressure and creation of mucosal oedema"* over the whole anterior cavity, so it does not need a target. Failing to see the spot is therefore a reason to pack anteriorly — and only when that pack fails does the situation become posterior.\n\n| Option | What it actually calls for | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Severe epistaxis in a shocked patient** | **anti-shock measures first** — the deck opens management with *"ABC’s… Vital signs — need IV?"*, and shock is treated before any pack (`enthd-nose-307`, `enthd-nose-295`). Shock says nothing about *where* the bleeding is | wrong |\n| **Failure of anterior packing** | the definition of posterior epistaxis; the next rung of the deck’s own ladder | **keyed** |\n| **Inability to localize the site** | an indication for an **anterior** pack, printed as such | wrong — and the commonest error here |\n| **All of the above** | two of the three belong to other manoeuvres | wrong |\n\n**Why the word *mandatory* matters: a posterior pack is not a step to be taken lightly.** The deck’s complication list is the longest in the lecture — *"Uncomfortable. **Hospitalization.** ET dysfunction and hemotympanum. **Hypoxia and cerebral ischemia. Myocardial infarction.** Toxic shock syndrome. **Failure rate = 20–50 %**"* — and its prerequisites read like an admission order: *"Elderly and those with other chronic diseases may need to be admitted to the **ICU**. Continuous cardiopulmonary monitoring. Antibiotics. Oxygen supplementation… Mild sedation/analgesia. IVF."* Something with a one-in-four to one-in-two failure rate and a risk of myocardial infarction is used when nothing simpler will do.\n\n**Bottom line: an anterior pack that has failed is the one situation that leaves no lesser option.** The balloon alternative is no gentler — *"Pressure in post. choana is not uniform. Balloon leakage. **Same disadvantages** as traditional post. nasal pack."*\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A posterior nasal pack becomes mandatory when an anterior pack has failed to stop the bleeding — inability to localize the bleeding site is an indication for an anterior pack, and shock calls for resuscitation first.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.87 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). The definition of posterior epistaxis, the posterior-management ladder, the three indications for an anterior pack, the posterior-pack complication list and prerequisites, the 20–50 % failure rate and the balloon slide are all transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-295', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'A **50-year-old hypertensive patient** presents with **severe epistaxis**. Which of the following is the **correct sequence** to treat this patient?',
  options:['Management of hypertension, anti-shock measures then anterior nasal packing','Anti-shock measures, anterior nasal packing then management of hypertension','Anterior nasal packing, management of hypertension then anti-shock measures','Anti-shock measures, management of hypertension then anterior nasal packing'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Resuscitate, then stop the bleeding, then treat the blood pressure — the patient is saved by circulating volume and haemostasis, not by a normal reading on the monitor.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` puts resuscitation at the head of its management slide and nothing before it: *"Epistaxis — initial management: **ABC’s.** Medical history/Medications. **Vital signs — need IV?** Head bent forward. Cold compresses. Spit out any blood."* Airway, breathing and circulation come before the nose, and the deck asks about intravenous access in the same breath as the vital signs.\n\n**Why haemostasis outranks the antihypertensive.** The deck ties this patient’s bleed to his blood pressure — *"The second peak is in the fifth or sixth decades and is **frequently associated with hypertension**"*, and *"Severe + elderly patient = hypertension"* — so treating the pressure is genuinely part of the treatment. But it is the **third** part, for two reasons:\n\n- **A bleeding, hypovolaemic patient who is given an antihypertensive can be tipped into hypotension**, and the pressure reading you lower may be the only thing perfusing the brain *(this pharmacological caution is not taken from the course material)*.\n- **A large part of the recorded pressure is pain, fear and catecholamine drive from the bleeding itself, and it falls once the nose is packed** *(also not taken from the course material)*. Treating the number before the cause treats an artefact.\n\n| Sequence offered | Where it goes wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **HTN → anti-shock → pack** | puts a drug before resuscitation, the exact inversion of *"ABC’s"* |\n| **Anti-shock → pack → HTN** | **keyed** — resuscitate, control the source, then remove the driver |\n| **Pack → HTN → anti-shock** | leaves a shocked patient unresuscitated while the nose is packed; a vasovagal attack during insertion (a listed pack complication) would then be uncorrectable |\n| **Anti-shock → HTN → pack** | the near-miss. Resuscitation is right, but it delays haemostasis to treat a number, and continues to lose blood while doing so |\n\n**What the anti-shock step contains.** The deck’s own furniture: IV access and fluids, the position that keeps blood out of the airway — *"The patient should be **sitting and leaning forward**, breathing through the mouth"* (Grade Gain keys the same position, `entqb-nose2-61`) — and the laboratory set for a severe bleed: *"CBC. Coagulation profile. Liver function tests. Creatinine. **Cross Match.**"* A cross-match is ordered at the resuscitation step, not after the pack.\n\n**Bottom line: ABC, then the bleeding point, then the blood pressure.** Cross-references: `enthd-nose-307` (anti-shock as the very first measure in severe epistaxis), `enthd-nose-301` (the same ladder in a child), `entep-nose-14` (why this patient’s hypertension also changes which pack material is safe).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'In severe epistaxis the order is anti-shock measures, then control of bleeding by anterior packing, then treatment of the hypertension that drives it — resuscitation first, the blood-pressure number last.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.87 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). *"ABC’s… Vital signs — need IV?"*, the bimodal age curve with hypertension, *"Severe + elderly patient = hypertension"*, the sitting-forward position and the laboratory list including cross-match are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`; the position is also Grade Gain’s printed key in `entqb-nose2-61`. **The two pharmacological reasons for deferring the antihypertensive are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-297', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'What is the **most feared complication of cautery of the ethmoidal arteries**?',
  options:['Cautery of external carotid artery or its other branches','Cautery of internal carotid artery or its other branches','Cautery of optic nerve due to its proximity to ethmoidal arteries','All of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Injury to the optic nerve — because the posterior ethmoidal artery lies only a few millimetres in front of the optic canal, and the price of that mistake is blindness.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` lists *"Anterior +/- posterior ethmoid ligation"* among the surgical options and *"Ethmoid coagulation"* among the endoscopic ones, but prints no complication list for the procedure. **The distances are printed by the other bank, and its printed answer is preferred here to outside knowledge:** `entqb-nose2-70` asks which distances must be considered *"to avoid damage to nearby structures"* when cauterising the ethmoidal artery, and keys **all** of them — *"24 mm to locate the anterior ethmoidal foramen and the anterior ethmoidal artery; 12 mm between the anterior and posterior ethmoidal arteries; **6 mm distance to the optic canal behind the posterior ethmoidal artery**"*.\n\nThat 24–12–6 sequence, measured back from the anterior lacrimal crest along the frontoethmoidal suture, is the whole safety margin of the operation. **Six millimetres is the width of the surgeon’s error bar**, and what sits at the end of it is the optic nerve entering its canal. Diathermy applied there — or heat conducted there — produces immediate, irreversible unilateral blindness in a patient who came in with a nosebleed.\n\n| Option | Why it is not the feared complication |\n|---|---|\n| **Cautery of the external carotid or its branches** | wrong vascular family. The ethmoidal arteries belong to the **internal** carotid system — `L25.1)` traces the anterior ethmoidal artery *"→ branch of the ophthalmic artery → branch of the **ICA**"*. The ECA branches to the nose (sphenopalatine, greater palatine) are reached at the sphenopalatine foramen, nowhere near the ethmoid foramina |\n| **Cautery of the internal carotid or its other branches** | the ethmoidal artery **is** an ICA-system branch, so coagulating one is the intention, not an accident; the ICA trunk itself lies in its canal and cavernous sinus, out of reach of an orbital or endonasal ethmoid approach |\n| **Optic nerve injury** | **keyed** — 6 mm away, and the only complication on this list that costs an organ |\n| **All of the above** | two of the three are anatomically impossible at this site | \n\n**⚠️ A second, related hazard worth knowing** *(not taken from the course material)*: a divided or slipped **anterior ethmoidal artery** can retract into the orbit and produce a **retrobulbar haematoma** — proptosis, a tense globe and rising intraocular pressure — which also blinds, and which is the emergency behind orbital decompression. Both hazards say the same thing: the ethmoidal arteries are **orbital** vessels that happen to feed the nose.\n\n**Bottom line: the roof of the nose is the floor of the orbit’s medial wall and the front door of the optic canal — ethmoidal surgery risks sight, not haemorrhage.** This is also why the deck prefers `enthd-nose-315`’s route, IMAX ligation and endoscopic SPA coagulation, for most posterior bleeds, and reserves ethmoid ligation for bleeding that is genuinely superior (`entqb-nose2-54`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The posterior ethmoidal artery lies about 6 mm in front of the optic canal, so the feared complication of ethmoidal artery cautery is optic nerve injury and blindness.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.88 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). Ethmoid ligation and ethmoid coagulation as surgical options, and the anterior ethmoidal artery traced to the ophthalmic artery and the ICA, are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`; superior epistaxis from the ethmoidal vessels is Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose2-54`. **No cached ENT deck prints the 24–12–6 mm distances or any complication of the procedure — they are taken from Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose2-70` in preference to outside knowledge. The retrobulbar haematoma is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-298', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'All of the following may cause epistaxis that is **resistant to conventional management except**?',
  options:['Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma','Epistaxis originating from septal branches of ethmoidal arteries','Epistaxis in a patient with a coagulation disorder','Epistaxis in a patient with sinusitis'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Sinusitis — it is the one cause on this list that bleeds from inflamed but otherwise normal mucosa, so ordinary measures work.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` files the causes of epistaxis in four groups, and the three resistant options come from three different groups, each resistant for its own reason:\n\n| Option | Its group on the deck’s list | Why conventional measures fail |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma** | local factor — *"Neoplasm"*; the deck also notes *"Middle age — tumors"* under age-related causes | a tumour, not a vessel. `L26) nasal masses.pdf` calls it a *"lobulated mass that arises in the confluence of posterolateral nasal wall and lateral nasopharynx"*, *"mainly supplied from **ECA (IMAX)**"*. Pressure cannot compress a vascular tumour that has no contractile wall, and the corpus keys **embolization** as the answer when packing fails in these patients (`entqb-nose7-221`, `entep-nose-42`) |\n| **Septal branches of the ethmoidal arteries** | the deck’s *"Bleeding areas"* slide names *"Ethmoidal vessels"* alongside Kiesselbach’s and Woodruff’s plexuses | this is **superior** epistaxis (`entqb-nose2-54`). It comes down from the roof, above the reach of an anterior pack, and it belongs to the **internal** carotid system — *"anterior ethmoidal artery → branch of the ophthalmic artery → branch of the ICA"* — so no amount of external carotid work reaches it. It needs its own operation, *"anterior +/- posterior ethmoid ligation"* (`enthd-nose-297`) |\n| **Coagulation disorder** | systemic factor — *"Bleeding disorders: Coagulopathies (hemophilia, anticoagulants). Thrombocytopenia (hypersplenism, DIC). Platelet dysfunction (leukemia, aspirin, Von Willebrand’s). Vasculitis (Osler-Weber)"* | packing and cautery both work by letting a clot form. A patient who cannot clot rebleeds around the pack and from every cautery burn; the deck orders a *"Coagulation profile"* for exactly this reason, and records that in children *"1/3 with chronic bleeds have coagulation d/o"* (`enthd-nose-299`) |\n| **Sinusitis** | local factor — *"Infectious/Inflammatory"* | **keyed as the exception.** Inflamed, hyperaemic mucosa oozes; the vessels are normal, the clotting is normal, and the bleeding is anterior and small. Nose pinching, a decongestant or cautery stops it, and treating the sinusitis stops it recurring |\n\n**The unifying principle is worth extracting: conventional management assumes a normal vessel in a normal patient in a reachable place.** Break any one of those three assumptions — an abnormal vessel (tumour), an abnormal patient (coagulopathy), or an unreachable place (the ethmoidal roof) — and the ladder of pinch, cautery and pack stops working. That is the same list the deck turns into `enthd-nose-292`’s indications for surgery and embolization.\n\n**Bottom line: sinusitis bleeds; angiofibromas, ethmoidal vessels and coagulopathies haemorrhage.** Grade Gain records mucopurulent discharge, not resistant bleeding, as the hallmark of rhinosinusitis, and `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` lists rhinosinusitis under mucopurulent discharge rather than under any bleeding heading.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Epistaxis resists conventional management when the vessel is abnormal (angiofibroma), the site is unreachable (ethmoidal/superior epistaxis) or the patient cannot clot (coagulopathy); bleeding from sinusitis is inflammatory ooze and responds to ordinary measures.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.88 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). The local/systemic/environmental aetiology lists, the bleeding-disorder breakdown, *"Middle age — tumors"*, the bleeding-areas slide naming the ethmoidal vessels, the ICA parentage of the anterior ethmoidal artery, the coagulation profile and *"1/3 with chronic bleeds have coagulation d/o"* are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`; the angiofibroma’s IMAX supply from `L26) nasal masses.pdf`; rhinosinusitis under mucopurulent discharge from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`. Embolization as the answer to packing failure in angiofibroma is the corpus’s printed key at `entqb-nose7-221` and `entep-nose-42`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-299', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'A **5-year-old child** presents with **recurrent attacks of epistaxis together with a haemorrhagic skin rash**. What is your **next step** in order to properly manage this child?',
  options:['CT nose and paranasal sinuses','CBC','ASO titer','Renal function tests'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**A CBC — bleeding at two separate sites in a child is a bleeding disorder until proved otherwise, and the platelet count is the first number that tells you which one.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` makes the paediatric link explicitly: *"Etiology and Age — **Children — foreign body, nose picking, (1/3 with chronic bleeds have coagulation d/o)**."* One child in three with recurrent nosebleeds has a coagulation disorder — and this child does not merely have recurrent nosebleeds, he has **bleeding into the skin as well**. A single-site bleed can be local; a two-site bleed is systemic.\n\nThe deck’s systemic list names the candidates, and every one of them shows on or starts with a blood count: *"Bleeding disorders — Coagulopathies (hemophilia, anticoagulants). **Thrombocytopenia** (hypersplenism, DIC). **Platelet dysfunction (leukemia**, aspirin, Von Willebrand’s). Vasculitis (Osler-Weber)."* Its investigation slide opens with the same test: *"**Lab Tests (For severe or recurrent epistaxis): CBC.** Coagulation profile. Liver function tests. Creatinine. Cross Match."* **CBC is printed first on the deck’s own list.**\n\n**What the CBC decides in one step** *(the differential is not taken from the course material)*: a low platelet count points to immune thrombocytopenia — the commonest cause of purpura plus epistaxis at this age; a low count with anaemia, a high or abnormal white count or blasts points to **leukaemia**, which the deck itself names; a normal count with normal cells sends you on to the coagulation profile for haemophilia or von Willebrand disease, and to the thought of a vasculitis with **normal** platelets.\n\n| Option | Why it is not the next step |\n|---|---|\n| **CT nose and paranasal sinuses** | images a **mass**, and this child has none of the features that would justify one — no unilateral obstruction, no unilateral discharge, no visible lesion. The corpus reserves CT for the adolescent male with a vascular nasopharyngeal mass (`entqb-nose7-229`, `entep-nose-78`). A CT also explains nothing about a skin rash |\n| **CBC** | **keyed** — the deck’s first-listed test, and the one investigation that covers thrombocytopenia, platelet dysfunction and leukaemia at once |\n| **ASO titer** | a marker of recent streptococcal infection. It has a place further down the road if a post-infectious vasculitis is being chased, but it neither counts platelets nor excludes leukaemia, and no course deck links it to epistaxis |\n| **Renal function tests** | *"Renal disease"* **is** on the deck’s systemic list and *"Creatinine"* **is** on its laboratory list — so this is the strongest distractor. But it sits **fourth** on that list, behind CBC; renal failure causes epistaxis through uraemic platelet dysfunction, which the CBC and the clinical picture reach first |\n\n**Bottom line: nosebleed plus purpura in a child is a haematological question, and the CBC is the cheapest answer to it.** ⚠️ **Note what this vignette is NOT:** the deck’s other paediatric causes — foreign body and nose picking — are **unilateral, local and unaccompanied by a rash** (`enthd-nose-308`, `enthd-nose-313`, `entqb-nose2-58`). The rash is the whole discriminator.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recurrent epistaxis with a haemorrhagic skin rash in a child indicates a systemic bleeding disorder — a third of children with chronic nosebleeds have one — and the CBC is the first investigation on the lecture’s own list.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.88 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). *"Children — foreign body, nose picking, (1/3 with chronic bleeds have coagulation d/o)"*, the systemic bleeding-disorder list including thrombocytopenia and leukaemia, *"Renal disease"* among systemic factors and the laboratory list beginning with CBC are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. CT reserved for a vascular nasopharyngeal mass is the corpus’s printed key at `entqb-nose7-229` and `entep-nose-78`. **The specific paediatric differential the CBC resolves (ITP, leukaemia, von Willebrand disease) is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material; the ASO titer is named by no cached ENT deck.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-300', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'**Bilateral cautery** of bleeding arteries in order to manage epistaxis can result in which of the following complications?',
  options:['Septal necrosis and perforation','Septal hematoma','Deviated septum','All of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Septal necrosis and perforation — burn the mucosa off both faces of the septum at the same spot and the cartilage between them starves.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` teaches the cautery itself — *"Silver nitrate sticks can be applied over the Little’s Area… lightly wiping the tip of the silver nitrate stick over the Little’s area until it becomes discoloured to a grey-white"* — but prints no complication for doing it on both sides. **The other bank prints the answer, and it is preferred here to outside knowledge:** `entqb-nose9-264` asks what *"silver nitrate cautery in epistaxis, when applied to **both sides of the nasal septum**"* can lead to, and keys **septal perforation**. `entqb-nose8-253` corroborates from the other direction, keying **hypertension** as the one thing on its list that does *not* cause septal perforation and leaving *"nasal surgery"* standing as one that does.\n\n**The mechanism is the anatomy of the septum** *(the perichondrial blood supply is not spelled out on any cached slide and is not taken from the course material)*. `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf` describes the quadrilateral cartilage as a plate *"3–4 mm thick"* with mucosa on each side; cartilage is avascular, and its entire nourishment diffuses in from the **mucoperichondrium** covering it. Destroy one side and the other side keeps the cartilage alive. Destroy **both sides at the same point** — which is precisely what simultaneous bilateral cautery does — and there is no route left: the cartilage necroses and a hole appears.\n\n| Option | What actually causes it | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Septal necrosis and perforation** | the bilateral burn, exactly as above | **keyed** |\n| **Septal haematoma** | trauma. `L26) nasal masses.pdf` defines it as *"the collection of blood **under the mucoperichondrium or mucoperiosteum** of nasal septal cartilage or bone… more frequent in the setting of severe trauma"*, and the corpus keys it for a man after nasal trauma without fever (`entqb-nose7-210`). Cautery seals vessels; it does not lift a flap and fill it with blood |\n| **Deviated septum** | developmental or traumatic — `L24)` gives *"Trauma (RTA, Sports) inflicted from front, side or below"* and buckling of the septum. A surface burn does not bend a cartilage plate |\n| **All of the above** | two of the three have unrelated mechanisms |\n| **None of the above** | contradicted by the other bank’s printed key |\n\n**Bottom line: cauterise one side at a time.** The practical rule that follows *(not taken from the course material)* is to treat one side, and if the other side must be treated too, to stagger it by four to six weeks or to offset the site so that the two burns never face each other across the cartilage. The clinical consequence of getting it wrong is the triad the corpus keys for a perforation — *"recurrent epistaxis, crustations, whistling"* (`entqb-nose8-258`) — plus the obstruction `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` lists under *"4. Septal perforation"*.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Simultaneous cautery of both sides of the nasal septum destroys the mucoperichondrial blood supply on which the avascular septal cartilage depends, causing septal necrosis and perforation.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.88 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). Straddles `ent-septum`; filed to `ent-epistaxis` because the question is about a complication of epistaxis treatment. The silver-nitrate technique is transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`; the 3–4 mm quadrilateral cartilage and traumatic septal deviation from `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf`; the definition of septal haematoma from `L26) nasal masses.pdf`; septal perforation among causes of nasal obstruction from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`. **No cached ENT deck names any complication of cautery — Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose9-264` (bilateral silver nitrate to the septum causing perforation) is preferred to outside knowledge, with `entqb-nose8-253` corroborating. The perichondrial nutrition of the cartilage and the stagger-the-sides rule are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. ⚠️ Five options, A–E.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-301', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'A **10-year-old boy** presents with **severe epistaxis from Little’s area, tachycardia and hypotension**. Which of the following is the **best management sequence**?',
  options:['Anti-shock measures, blood transfusion, anterior nasal packing then cautery','Anti-shock measures, ligation of ECA then anterior nasal packing','Anterior nasal packing, anti-shock measures then ligation of ECA','None of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Resuscitate, replace what he has lost, control the bleeding with a pack, and only then treat the bleeding point — the same ladder as in the adult, with the transfusion made explicit because a child’s blood volume is small.**\n\n**Step 1 — anti-shock measures.** Tachycardia with hypotension in a child is decompensated hypovolaemic shock, and `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` puts resuscitation before everything: *"Epistaxis — initial management: **ABC’s.** Medical history/Medications. **Vital signs — need IV?**"*, with the patient *"sitting and leaning forward, breathing through the mouth"* and told to *"spit out any blood"* so that swallowed blood is not mistaken for continued stability.\n\n**Step 2 — blood transfusion.** The deck orders a **cross-match** as part of the work-up of severe epistaxis — *"Lab tests: CBC. Coagulation profile. Liver functions tests. Creatinine. **Cross Match**"* — and it treats the transfusion requirement as a threshold serious enough to justify an operation: *"Pt requires transfusion/admit hct of <38 %"* sits among its **indications for surgery/embolization** (`enthd-nose-292`).\n\n**Step 3 — anterior nasal packing.** The bleeding is *"from Little’s area"*, i.e. anterior and septal, *"the usual site for epistaxis in children and young adults"*. An anterior pack is the manoeuvre that matches that site.\n\n**Step 4 — cautery.** Once bleeding is controlled and the spot is visible and dry, cautery treats the source and prevents the next attack. That order — control, then cauterise — is the whole point of `enthd-nose-290`.\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Anti-shock → transfusion → anterior pack → cautery** | **keyed** |\n| **Anti-shock → ECA ligation → anterior pack** | takes a 10-year-old with a **visible anterior septal** bleed straight to major surgery, and to the one operation the deck marks *"**Obsolete**"* on its 2023 slide. It also ligates after nothing has been tried, when the deck’s indications for surgery all require packing to have failed first |\n| **Anterior pack → anti-shock → ECA ligation** | packs a shocked child before resuscitating him — the inversion of *"ABC’s"* — and then escalates to an obsolete operation |\n| **None of the above** | option A is a coherent ladder |\n\n**⚠️ One tension in the key, recorded and not disputed:** the sequence transfuses **before** the pack, whereas many would pack first on the principle that stopping the loss precedes replacing it. The key is defensible because the two run in parallel in practice — the pack goes in while the blood is being cross-matched and hung — and because the deck itself groups cross-match with the initial laboratory work-up rather than with definitive treatment. **The printed key stands.**\n\n**Bottom line: in a shocked child, treat the circulation and the bleeding at the same time, and the bleeding POINT last.** Cross-references: `enthd-nose-295` (identical logic in a hypertensive adult), `enthd-nose-307` (anti-shock as the first measure of all), `enthd-nose-314` (why a posterior pack has no place in a child’s primary epistaxis).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Severe epistaxis with tachycardia and hypotension is resuscitated and transfused first, then controlled by anterior packing appropriate to a Little’s area bleed, and cauterised last; external carotid ligation is obsolete and never a first-line step in a child.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.88 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). *"ABC’s… Vital signs — need IV?"*, the sitting-forward position, the laboratory list with cross-match, *"Pt requires transfusion/admit hct of <38 %"* among the indications for surgery, Little’s area as *"the usual site for epistaxis in children and young adults"* and *"External carotid ligation — Obsolete"* are all transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. **⚠️ Tension recorded, key held: the sequence transfuses before packing; the note is in the explanation and the printed key is kept.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-302', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most common cause of anosmia**?',
  options:['Post-viral','Traumatic','1ry atrophic rhinitis','Frontal lobe masses'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Trauma — head injury heads the lecture’s own frequency table, and it is the only number on it that reaches 19 %.**\n\n`L25.2) smell.pdf` closes its causes section with a percentage table, and every question in this trio is answered off it:\n\n| Cause | Share |\n|---|---|\n| **Head injury** | **19 %** |\n| Post URI | 17 % |\n| Idiopathic-nasal | 17 |\n| Nasal/sinus disease | 16 % |\n| Toxic exposure-nasal | 5 |\n| Multiple | 5 |\n| Congenital | 2 |\n| Age | 1 |\n\n**⚠️ This item is the first of a deliberate three-question set printed on the same menu, and the three must never be merged.** `enthd-nose-303` asks for the **2nd** most common cause and keys **post-viral**; `enthd-nose-304` asks for the most common **sensorineural** cause and also keys **post-viral**, but reaches it by a different route. The shared frequency table lives here; the siblings point at it.\n\n**Why trauma damages smell so readily.** The deck devotes a slide to it: *"severe blows to the head, concussion or **minor head trauma**… occasionally associated with a fracture running through the nose and the **cribiform plate**… damage to the **olfactory bulbs**… **shearing or stretching of the olfactory nerves at the cribiform plate, which occurs in approximately 10 % of major head injuries**."* The olfactory filaments pass through a perforated sheet of bone into a bulb tethered to the brain; the bone and the brain move at different rates in a deceleration, and the filaments tear. One in ten major head injuries does it.\n\n| Option | Its place on the deck’s list | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Post-viral** | *"Post URI 17 %"* — second, and the deck’s exemplar of a viral neuritis: *"a viral infection… may damage the olfactory nerve… the viral particles destroy the normal functioning of the nasal olfactory cells"* | second, not first (`enthd-nose-303`) |\n| **Traumatic** | *"Head injury 19 %"* — first | **keyed** |\n| **1ry atrophic rhinitis** | a cause, but a small one; it belongs inside *"Nasal/sinus disease 16 %"*. `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` describes *"progressive atrophy of nasal mucosa and the underlying bone of the turbinates"* with crusts *"that emit a characteristic foul odor (ozaena)"* — and the anosmia that goes with it is famous but uncommon (`enthd-nose-312`) |\n| **Frontal lobe masses** | rarest of all. A subfrontal meningioma or olfactory groove tumour compresses the bulb and tract, but such tumours are rare and do not appear on the deck’s table at all *(their existence as a cause is not taken from the course material)* |\n\n**Bottom line: the commonest way to lose smell is to hit your head.** The prognostic consequence is the reason the distinction matters — traumatic anosmia from sheared filaments recovers poorly, whereas post-viral loss recovers well, and Grade Gain keys **postviral anosmia** as the type *"most commonly associated with good recovery"* (`entqb-nose2-79`; `entep-nose-47` keys post-viral as the most common **reversible** cause).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Head injury is the single commonest cause of anosmia at 19 % on the lecture’s frequency table, through shearing of the olfactory filaments at the cribriform plate — which happens in about 10 % of major head injuries.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.89 (Nose ch.3, smell disorders). The full percentage table, the trauma slide with the cribriform-plate shearing and its 10 % figure, and the viral-neuritis slide are transcribed from `L25.2) smell.pdf`; atrophic rhinitis and ozaena from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`. Post-viral loss as the most recoverable type is Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose2-79` and the corpus’s `entep-nose-47`. **⚠️ First of the deliberate trio Q302/Q303/Q304 recorded in the sweep — do not merge. The shared frequency table lives in this entry; `enthd-nose-303` and `enthd-nose-304` point at it. Frontal lobe masses are named by no cached deck and are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-303', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **2nd most common cause of anosmia**?',
  options:['Post-viral','Traumatic','1ry atrophic rhinitis','Frontal lobe masses'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Post-viral — 17 % on the lecture’s table, one rank and two percentage points behind head injury.**\n\nThe full frequency table is set out in `enthd-nose-302` and is not repeated here. Its top three are *"Head injury 19 %, **Post URI 17 %**, Nasal/sinus disease 16 %"*, so with trauma taken by the first question of the trio, **post-viral is the second place on the list.**\n\n**⚠️ One reading of the table has to be dealt with, because the deck prints a tie.** *"Idiopathic-nasal 17"* carries the same figure as *"Post URI 17 %"*. It does not displace post-viral here for two reasons: **idiopathic is not offered as an option**, and idiopathic is not a cause at all but the residue left when no cause is found. Among **named** causes, post-viral is second. **This is recorded so that the reader who counts the table is not left thinking the key is loose.**\n\n**What the deck teaches about the mechanism.** `L25.2) smell.pdf`: *"A viral infection or respiratory virus may damage the **olfactory nerve**. When a viral infection like the ‘flu’ involves the cells of the **olfactory epithelium**, the viral particles destroy the normal functioning of the nasal olfactory cells."* It adds a second, mechanical route on the same slide — *"**violent coughing** can damage the nerves in the olfactory glands"* — so a URI can take smell twice over.\n\n| Option | Rank on the deck’s table | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Post-viral** | *"Post URI 17 %"* — second among named causes | **keyed** |\n| **Traumatic** | *"Head injury 19 %"* — **first**, and the key of `enthd-nose-302` | wrong rank, not wrong fact |\n| **1ry atrophic rhinitis** | subsumed in *"Nasal/sinus disease 16 %"*, third at best and only a fraction of it | too low |\n| **Frontal lobe masses** | absent from the table entirely | rarest |\n\n**⚠️ Do not merge this item with `enthd-nose-304`, which keys the same option.** That question restricts the field to **sensorineural** anosmia before asking, and arrives at post-viral by a different argument entirely; the reasoning is set out there. Two questions sharing a key on one menu is deliberate here, and is the trio’s teaching device.\n\n**Bottom line: trauma first, virus second — and the second is the one that gets better.** The deck’s COVID slides give post-viral loss its prognosis: *"Improvement in 6 W — 90 %"*, and Grade Gain keys postviral anosmia as the type most associated with good recovery (`entqb-nose2-79`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Post-viral loss is the second commonest cause of anosmia at 17 % on the lecture’s table, behind head injury at 19 % — viral particles destroy olfactory epithelial cell function, and violent coughing adds a mechanical insult.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.89 (Nose ch.3, smell disorders). The percentage table, the viral-neuritis slide, *"violent coughing can damage the nerves in the olfactory glands"* and *"Improvement in 6 W — 90 %"* are transcribed from `L25.2) smell.pdf`. Good recovery of postviral anosmia is Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose2-79`. **⚠️ Second of the deliberate trio Q302/Q303/Q304 — do not merge with either sibling; the shared table lives in `enthd-nose-302` and the sensorineural argument in `enthd-nose-304`. The tie with *"Idiopathic-nasal 17"* is recorded in the explanation and the key is held.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-304', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most common cause of SENSORINEURAL anosmia**?',
  options:['Post-viral','Traumatic','1ry atrophic rhinitis','Frontal lobe masses'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Post-viral — and it wins this question by a different argument from the one that made it second in `enthd-nose-303`.**\n\n**⚠️ Third of the deliberate trio. `enthd-nose-303` and this item key the same option on the same menu, and that is correct, not a duplicate.** The difference is the question, and therefore the route:\n\n| | The question asks | The field it is ranked in | The route to the answer |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `enthd-nose-303` | 2nd most common cause of anosmia **overall** | every cause, conductive and sensorineural together | **counting the deck’s frequency table**: 19 % trauma, then **17 % post URI** |\n| **this item** | most common cause of **sensorineural** anosmia | only the sensorineural half | **classifying first, then ranking** — and the deck’s exemplar of sensorineural loss is the virus |\n\n**Step one: the deck’s own classification.** `L25.2) smell.pdf` divides anosmia into two mechanisms before it lists a single cause:\n\n> *"**Conductive** — Physical obstacle of odorant to reach olfactory epithelium. Rheological properties of mucus. **Sensorineural** — **Impairment of olfactory epithelium function. Disrupted neuronal pathway.**"*\n\n**Step two: the deck names the exemplar of each.** Its illustrated slide *"Smell disorder … anosmia"* prints exactly two labels — **AR** and **Viral** — the allergic nose against the infected one, and its comparison table makes the mechanism explicit: **AR** has *"**No receptor damage**"* and is *"usually reversible & recurrent"* with *"Dysosmia +"*, while **Covid-19** has *"**+/- receptor damage, neuritis**"*, is *"reversible (neuritis) / **irreversible (receptor damage)**"* with *"Dysosmia +++"*. Receptor damage and neuritis are the two words of the sensorineural definition. **The virus is the deck’s sensorineural cause; allergic rhinitis is its conductive one** (`enthd-nose-306`).\n\n**Step three, and the honest part: trauma is sensorineural too, and it heads the raw table.** `L25.2)` describes *"shearing or stretching of the olfactory nerves at the cribiform plate"* — a disrupted neuronal pathway by any reading — at 19 % against post URI’s 17 %. **Three things narrow and then reverse that gap, and they are recorded rather than assumed:**\n\n- **Part of the trauma share is conductive, not sensorineural.** The same deck’s structural slide gives *"**Deviated nasal septum, crooked nose** — can physically impede air flow through the nose, so that odors are not available to the olfactory epithelium"*, and a crooked nose is usually a broken one. Post-viral loss has no conductive fraction to subtract.\n- **The table predates COVID.** The deck’s own later slides record *"Covid-19 … new symptoms — **New loss of smell +/- taste**, July 2020"* and *"**Sudden loss of smell (LOS) 50 %**"* of cases. A virus that takes smell from half the people it infects, during a pandemic, is not represented in a pre-2020 clinic series.\n- **The other bank keys it outright, and its printed answer is preferred to reasoning:** `entqb-nose2-78` asks for the most common cause of **perceptive** anosmia — the same class under its other name — and keys *"Postviral infection"*.\n\n| Option | Class | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Post-viral** | sensorineural — receptor damage and neuritis | **keyed** |\n| **Traumatic** | sensorineural, but partly conductive, and its 19 % is a pre-COVID figure | the recorded tension, above |\n| **1ry atrophic rhinitis** | **conductive by mechanism** — crusts and altered mucus, the deck’s *"rheological properties of mucus"*; the anosmia of atrophic rhinitis is famous but the disease is uncommon (`enthd-nose-312`) | wrong class |\n| **Frontal lobe masses** | sensorineural by mechanism, absent from the deck’s table, rare | too rare |\n\n**Bottom line: ask *which mechanism* before asking *how common*, and post-viral is the answer to the sensorineural half every time.** It is also the half with the best outlook — *"Improvement in 6 W — 90 %"* — treated by *"olfactory training"* with *"topical nasal steroids… omega 3, Vit A drops"*, with the warning that *"oral steroids are contraindicated in the acute stage of the disease"*.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Post-viral infection is the commonest sensorineural (perceptive) anosmia, because the virus damages olfactory receptors and causes neuritis — the deck’s own model of sensorineural loss, against allergic rhinitis as the conductive one.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.89 (Nose ch.3, smell disorders). The conductive/sensorineural definitions, the AR-versus-Viral slide, the AR-versus-Covid comparison table, the trauma and structural-change slides, the COVID symptom and 50 % LOS slides, *"Improvement in 6 W — 90 %"* and the olfactory-training regimen are all transcribed from `L25.2) smell.pdf`. Perceptive anosmia keyed to postviral infection is Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose2-78`. **⚠️ Third of the deliberate trio Q302/Q303/Q304 — shares its key with `enthd-nose-303` by design and must not be merged with it; the two routes are tabulated in the explanation. ⚠️ The tension with trauma’s higher raw percentage is recorded and the printed key is held.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-305', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most common cause of PERCEPTIVE anosmia**?',
  options:['Covid 19','Influenza','Allergic rhinitis','Common cold'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Covid-19 — of the three viruses on this menu it is the one the lecture singles out, and it takes smell from half the people it infects.**\n\n*Perceptive* is the other name for **sensorineural**: `L25.2) smell.pdf` defines it as *"impairment of olfactory epithelium function; disrupted neuronal pathway"*, against **conductive** loss, which is *"physical obstacle of odorant to reach olfactory epithelium"*. So this question and `enthd-nose-306` are the same question asked twice, once for each mechanism, on one menu.\n\n**What the deck gives COVID that it gives no other virus.** An entire section: *"Covid-19 … new symptoms — Generalized body aches and headache; **New loss of smell +/- taste**; GIT manifestations — July 2020"*, then *"Covid-19 … anosmia — **Sudden loss of smell (LOS) 50 %.** Improvement in 6 W — 90 %. **Positive Covid need no further investigations.**"* Half of all infections, sudden onset, and a diagnosis so secure that the deck waives further work-up. Its comparison table then places COVID unambiguously in the perceptive class — *"+/- **receptor damage, neuritis**"*, *"reversible (neuritis) / irreversible (receptor damage)"*, *"**Dysosmia +++**"* — while allergic rhinitis has *"**no receptor damage**"*.\n\n| Option | Mechanism | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Covid 19** | perceptive — receptor damage and olfactory neuritis, sudden and often isolated | **keyed** |\n| **Influenza** | genuinely perceptive, and the deck’s generic example — *"when a viral infection like the **‘flu’** involves the cells of the olfactory epithelium, the viral particles destroy the normal functioning of the nasal olfactory cells"* — but nothing in the deck ranks influenza first, and it has no slide of its own | a real cause, outranked |\n| **Allergic rhinitis** | **conductive** — the wrong class entirely, and the key of `enthd-nose-306` | wrong class |\n| **Common cold** | mostly **conductive**: congestion and secretions block the odorant’s path, and smell returns as the nose clears. `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` files *"early stages of acute viral rhinitis (common cold)"* under **watery discharge**, i.e. as an obstructive picture | mainly the wrong class, and transient |\n\n**⚠️ A CROSS-BANK POINT THAT IS NOT A DIVERGENCE, AND IS WORTH THE SPACE.** Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose2-78` asks this identical question — *"What is the most common cause of perceptive anosmia?"* — and keys **"Postviral infection"**. This bank keys **Covid 19**. **The two keys agree, because the menus differ:** Grade Gain offers *allergic rhinitis / postviral infection / chronic sinusitis / nasal polyps*, a menu with one viral option and three obstructive ones, so the answer must be the general category. This bank offers **three named viruses and one allergy**, so the general category is already assumed and the question becomes *which virus* — and the deck answers COVID. **COVID-19 is a postviral infection; each bank keys the best answer available in its own option list.**\n\n**Bottom line: perceptive = receptor damage = virus, and among viruses the deck’s named answer is COVID.** Treatment follows the class — *"olfactory training"*, *"steroid drops / topical nasal steroids"*, *"omega 3, Vit A drops"*, and *"oral steroids are contraindicated in the acute stage"* — and the deck’s triage question for any loss of smell is *"Isolated or not? Covid-19 or not? Duration < 2 W or more?"*\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Perceptive (sensorineural) anosmia is caused by receptor damage and olfactory neuritis, and COVID-19 is its commonest named cause — sudden loss of smell in 50 % of infections, with 90 % improving within six weeks.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.89 (Nose ch.3, smell disorders). The conductive/sensorineural definitions, the COVID symptom slides, *"Sudden loss of smell (LOS) 50 %"*, *"Improvement in 6 W — 90 %"*, *"Positive Covid need no further investigations"*, the AR-versus-Covid table, the *"‘flu’"* slide, the triage questions and the treatment slide are all transcribed from `L25.2) smell.pdf`; the common cold under watery discharge from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`. **⚠️ Cross-bank note recorded, NOT a divergence: Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose2-78` asks the identical question and keys *"Postviral infection"* on a menu that offers no named virus, while this bank offers three named viruses and keys the one its own deck singles out. Both keys are right within their own option lists. ⚠️ Deliberate contrast pair with Q306 (conductive) — do not merge.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-306', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most common cause of CONDUCTIVE anosmia**?',
  options:['Covid 19','Influenza','Allergic rhinitis','Common cold'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Allergic rhinitis — the odorant never reaches the olfactory epithelium, and the epithelium itself is untouched.**\n\n`L25.2) smell.pdf` defines the class in two lines: *"**Conductive** — **Physical obstacle of odorant to reach olfactory epithelium.** **Rheological properties of mucus.**"* Allergic rhinitis supplies both halves at once — the turbinates swell and block the airstream from reaching the olfactory cleft high in the nose, and the watery hypersecretion changes the mucus the odorant must dissolve in before it can be smelled at all.\n\n**The deck then names AR as its conductive exemplar twice.** Its illustrated slide prints only two labels, *"**AR**"* and *"**Viral**"*, one for each mechanism; and its comparison table gives AR the signature of a conductive loss: *"**No receptor damage**"*, *"usually **reversible & recurrent**"*, *"Dysosmia **+**"* — against Covid-19’s *"+/- receptor damage, neuritis"*, *"irreversible (receptor damage)"*, *"Dysosmia +++"*. **No receptor damage is the definition of a conductive loss.** *Recurrent* is the other giveaway: smell comes and goes with the season and with the swelling.\n\n**Grade Gain keys the identical question the identical way, and its printed answer is preferred to reasoning:** `entqb-nose2-77`, *"What is the most common cause of conductive anosmia?"* → *"**allergic rhinitis**"*, ahead of chronic sinusitis, head trauma and olfactory nerve damage.\n\n| Option | Mechanism | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Covid 19** | **perceptive** — receptor damage and neuritis; the key of `enthd-nose-305`, the deliberate mirror of this item | wrong class |\n| **Influenza** | perceptive — *"the viral particles destroy the normal functioning of the nasal olfactory cells"* | wrong class |\n| **Allergic rhinitis** | conductive — obstruction plus altered mucus, receptors intact | **keyed** |\n| **Common cold** | the strongest distractor, and genuinely conductive while it lasts: congestion blocks the olfactory cleft, and `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` files *"early stages of acute viral rhinitis (common cold)"* under watery discharge. It loses on **duration and repetition** — a cold blocks the nose for a few days and resolves, whereas allergic rhinitis is *"usually reversible & recurrent"* over years and is, with viral infection, one of *"the commonest causes of nasal obstruction (in adults)"* (`L31)`). A cold that lasts long enough to damage the epithelium has stopped being conductive and become the post-viral loss of `enthd-nose-304` | outranked, and it changes class if it persists |\n\n**Bottom line: conductive anosmia is a plumbing problem — unblock the nose and smell returns; perceptive anosmia is a wiring problem, and it may not.** That is why the treatment differs entirely: an antihistamine and a topical steroid for the allergic nose, versus olfactory training with topical steroids, omega-3 and vitamin A drops for the post-viral one — and *"oral steroids are contraindicated in the acute stage"* of COVID. ⚠️ **Do not merge with `enthd-nose-305`; the pair exists to contrast the two mechanisms on one option list.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Conductive anosmia is a physical obstacle to the odorant plus altered mucus with intact receptors, and allergic rhinitis is its commonest cause — recurrent, reversible, and marked by no receptor damage.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.89 (Nose ch.3, smell disorders). The conductive definition, the AR-versus-Viral slide, the AR-versus-Covid comparison table, the *"‘flu’"* slide and the treatment slide are transcribed from `L25.2) smell.pdf`; the common cold under watery discharge and *"nasal allergy and viral infections are the commonest causes of nasal obstruction (in adults)"* from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`. Allergic rhinitis as the commonest conductive anosmia is also Grade Gain’s printed key in `entqb-nose2-77`. **⚠️ Deliberate contrast pair with Q305 (perceptive) — do not merge.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-307', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **first measure** to be carried out in case of **severe epistaxis**?',
  options:['Anterior nasal packing','Posterior nasal packing','Cautery','Arterial clipping','Anti-shock measures'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**Anti-shock measures — the lecture’s management slide begins with the letters ABC, and everything else on this list treats the nose rather than the patient.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` prints the order plainly: *"Epistaxis — initial management: **ABC’s.** Medical history/Medications. **Vital signs — need IV?** Head bent forward. Cold compresses. Spit out any blood."* Airway, breathing and circulation first; intravenous access considered in the same line as the observations; and only then anything aimed at the bleeding point.\n\n**The word *severe* is what makes this question have an answer at all.** In an ordinary nosebleed the first measure is first aid — *"direct digital pressure to the lower soft cartilaginous part of the nose by **pinching for approximately 10 minutes** is the method of choice"*, with the patient *"sitting and leaning forward, breathing through the mouth"*. In a **severe** bleed the patient may be hypovolaemic before you reach the nose, and the deck’s laboratory list for severe or recurrent epistaxis ends with *"**Cross Match**"* — a test ordered for a patient you expect to transfuse.\n\n| Option | Where it belongs in the ladder | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Anterior nasal packing** | after first aid fails, and when *"cautery is not available… cautery fails… bleeding site is not identified"* | later |\n| **Posterior nasal packing** | only after an anterior pack has failed (`enthd-nose-294`) — and it needs hospitalization, monitoring and sometimes ICU | much later |\n| **Cautery** | after control, when the bleeding point is visible and dry (`enthd-nose-290`) | later |\n| **Arterial clipping** | after packing fails, per the deck’s indications for surgery/embolization (`enthd-nose-292`, `enthd-nose-315`) | last |\n| **Anti-shock measures** | *"ABC’s"* — before all of them | **keyed** |\n\n**⚠️ Note the shape of the whole list: it is the deck’s escalation ladder printed as an option set, with the resuscitation step hidden at position E.** That is the trap. Every other option is a real and correct step in severe epistaxis — just not the **first** one. A candidate who reads the question as *"what treats the bleeding"* picks A; a candidate who reads it as *"what treats the patient"* picks E.\n\n**Bottom line: the nose is never the first thing you treat in a patient who is losing blood fast.** This is the same principle worked through as a sequence in `enthd-nose-295` (hypertensive adult) and `enthd-nose-301` (shocked child). ⚠️ **Five options are printed, A–E; the key is E.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'In severe epistaxis the first measure is resuscitation — ABC, vital signs and intravenous access — before any packing, cautery or arterial ligation.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.90 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). *"ABC’s… Vital signs — need IV?"*, the nose-pinching first-aid slide with its ten minutes and sitting-forward position, the indications for an anterior pack and the laboratory list ending in cross-match are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. **⚠️ Five options, A–E; the key is E.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-308', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-nasalobs',
  stem:'**Unilateral offensive nasal discharge in a 4-year-old boy** is suggestive of which of the following?',
  options:['Angiofibroma','SCC','Allergic rhinitis','Impacted foreign body'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**An impacted foreign body — the lecture states this one outright as a true/false item, and both other banks key it too.**\n\n`L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf` prints it as a statement to be marked **true**: *"**Foreign bodies in the nose is the most common cause of unilateral nasal discharge with bad odor in children.**"* `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` gives the differential it heads: *"**Nasal Discharge: Foetid** — 1. **Foreign bodies in the nose.** 2. Odontogenic sinusitis. 3. Sinonasal neoplasms. 4. Fungal infections."* Of those four, only one belongs to a four-year-old, and the same deck lists *"3. Foreign bodies"* among the *"causes of nasal obstruction in children"*.\n\n**Why the discharge smells.** An impacted object obstructs one side; secretions stagnate behind it, become infected and putrefy, and the surrounding mucosa granulates — `L26) nasal masses.pdf` files both *"FB"* and *"FB granuloma"* among **unilateral** nasal masses. **Unilateral + foetid + child is the triad**, and each word does work: bilateral would suggest a mucosal cause, non-offensive would suggest simple rhinitis, and an adult would open the differential to tumour and odontogenic disease.\n\n| Option | The patient it belongs to | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Angiofibroma** | an **adolescent male**, presenting with *"nasal obstruction (unilateral/bilateral)"* and **epistaxis** — `L26)` calls it a *"lobulated mass… mainly supplied from ECA (IMAX)"*. It bleeds; it does not produce offensive pus, and it does not occur at four | wrong age, wrong symptom |\n| **SCC** | an elderly smoker. The corpus keys sinonasal malignancy for a *"60-year-old male… smoker"* with unilateral obstruction, epistaxis and cheek numbness (`entep-nose-2`), and `L26)` lists *"persistent unilateral progressive nasal obstruction… persistent unilateral epistaxis"* among its red flags | wrong age by half a century |\n| **Allergic rhinitis** | **bilateral and watery**, not unilateral and foetid — `L31)` files allergic rhinitis under *"Nasal Discharge: Watery — **Bilateral**"* | wrong laterality and wrong character |\n| **Impacted foreign body** | **keyed** | |\n\n**⚠️ The corpus prints this vignette twice more with the same key**, which is as strong a confirmation as this project gets: `entep-nose-18` gives a *"4-year-old child… foul-smelling discharge from his left nostril"* and keys **nasal foreign body**; `entqb-nose2-84` keys **foreign body** for unilateral epistaxis in a child.\n\n**Bottom line: in a small child, one smelly nostril is a foreign body until it has been looked at.** The same object bleeds as well as discharges, which is the vignette at `enthd-nose-313`; and it is one of the two paediatric causes of epistaxis the epistaxis deck names — *"Children — **foreign body**, nose picking"* (`L25.1)`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Unilateral foetid nasal discharge in a young child is an impacted nasal foreign body — the lecture names it the commonest cause, and it heads the differential of foetid discharge.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.90 (Nose ch.3). Straddles `ent-nasalmass`; filed to `ent-nasalobs` to match the corpus’s filing of the identical vignette at `entep-nose-18`. *"Foreign bodies in the nose is the most common cause of unilateral nasal discharge with bad odor in children"* is transcribed from `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`; the foetid- and watery-discharge lists and the paediatric causes of obstruction from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`; *"FB granuloma"*, the angiofibroma description and the unilateral red flags from `L26) nasal masses.pdf`; *"Children — foreign body, nose picking"* from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. The same key is printed by the corpus at `entep-nose-18` and `entqb-nose2-84`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-309', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'In **anosmia of organic cause**, which of the following substances **can still be detected** by the patient?',
  options:['Coffee','Garlic','Onion','Ammonia'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Ammonia — it is not really smelled at all. It is felt, by the trigeminal nerve, and the trigeminal nerve is not what anosmia destroys.**\n\nThe nose carries two separate chemical senses. `L25.2) smell.pdf` traces the olfactory one from end to end — *"Olfactory neurons → Olfactory bulb → Lateral olfactory tract → Primary olfactory cortex (pyriform area)"*, with thalamus and hypothalamus alongside — and it flags the second one in its diagnostic list: *"**TG assessment. TG receptors can alter olfactory receptors.**"* TG is the trigeminal. `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf` confirms that the nasal mucosa is trigeminal territory when it explains sneezing as *"triggered by nasal irritation (**stimulation of the trigeminal nerve**)"*.\n\n**Ammonia, menthol, capsaicin and strong acids are irritants**, and they excite free trigeminal endings throughout the nasal mucosa — producing burning, stinging and a reflex urge to withdraw rather than a recognised odour *(the pharmacology of trigeminal chemesthesis is not taken from the course material)*. Coffee, garlic and onion are **pure odorants**: they have no irritant component at ordinary strength and are detected only by olfactory receptor neurons in a 5 cm² patch high in the nose — the deck’s *"5 cm² in humans"* olfactory area, with *"sensory cells, Bowman’s glands, pigment cells"*.\n\n| Option | Sense that detects it | In organic anosmia |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Coffee** | CN I only | not detected |\n| **Garlic** | CN I only | not detected |\n| **Onion** | CN I only — the lacrimation onions cause is a chemical released on cutting reaching the **eye**, not a nasal trigeminal response to sniffing | not detected |\n| **Ammonia** | **CN V**, trigeminal irritant | **still detected — keyed** |\n\n**⚠️ Both other banks print this same question with the same key**, which is why no outside source is needed for the fact itself: `entep-nose-108` — *"a patient with anosmia will still respond to inhalation of"* → **ammonia**; `entqb-nose2-80` — *"a patient with anosmia cannot smell odors, but he/she could perceive"* → **ammonia**.\n\n**Why the stem says ORGANIC, and why that word is the whole clinical point.** In organic anosmia the olfactory pathway is damaged and the trigeminal is intact, so ammonia is still felt. In **non-organic** loss — malingering, or the *"hysteric conversion reactions"* the deck lists among its psychiatric causes alongside *"depression… and schizophrenia"* — the patient believes ammonia is something one smells, and denies feeling it too. **A patient who denies ammonia has over-claimed, and that is the bedside test.** The formal versions of the same idea are on the deck’s testing slides: the olfactometer, *"smell threshold test"*, *"smell testing (UPSIT)"*, the electro-olfactogram and olfactory event-related potentials.\n\n**Bottom line: smell is CN I, irritation is CN V, and only one of them is lost in anosmia.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Ammonia is a trigeminal irritant rather than a pure odorant, so it is still perceived in organic anosmia — denial of ammonia points to a non-organic or feigned loss.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.90 (Nose ch.3, smell disorders). The olfactory pathway, the 5 cm² olfactory area, *"TG assessment. TG receptors can alter olfactory receptors"*, the psychiatric causes including hysteric conversion reactions, and the smell-testing slides are transcribed from `L25.2) smell.pdf`; sneezing as trigeminal nasal irritation from `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`. **Ammonia as the substance still perceived is the printed key of both other banks — `entep-nose-108` and `entqb-nose2-80` — and is preferred to outside knowledge; the trigeminal chemesthesis mechanism is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-310', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'Which of the following is **true regarding smell disorders**?',
  options:['Olfactory agnosia is mostly observed in cases of strokes','Presbyosmia is a normal condition accompanying aging','Hyperosmia is often observed in cases of szhizophrenia','All of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**All three are true, and each one names a level of the olfactory system: the cortex, the receptor sheet, and the brain’s interpretation of what arrives.**\n\n`L25.2) smell.pdf` supplies the vocabulary and the pathway; **the three terms in the stem are not themselves printed by any cached ENT deck, so each is grounded below and tagged where the deck runs out.**\n\n> *"**Osme** is the Greek word meaning odor. It is the stem word for many medical definitions relating to smell."* — *"ANOSMIA: a complete loss of smell. HYPOSMIA: partial loss of smell. **HYPEROSMIA: enhanced smell sensitivity.** DYSOSMIA: distortion in odour perception (includes parosmia & phantosmia). PAROSMIA: distortion of perception of external stimulus. PHANTOSMIA: smell perception with no external stimulus."*\n\n| Statement | Grounding | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Olfactory agnosia in strokes** | the deck’s pathway slide runs *"olfactory neurons → olfactory bulb → lateral olfactory tract → **primary olfactory cortex (pyriform area)**"*, with thalamus and hypothalamus. Agnosia is a lesion of the **last** station: the odour is detected normally but cannot be recognised or named — a cortical, not a nasal, deficit, and stroke is the commonest way to lose a piece of cortex suddenly *(the term "olfactory agnosia" and its attribution to stroke are not taken from the course material; the pathway that makes it possible is the deck’s own)* | **true** |\n| **Presbyosmia with aging** | the deck’s frequency table lists *"**Age 1**"* as a cause of smell loss in its own right — the same construction as presbycusis for hearing, and the reason smell testing is normed by age. Receptor neurons are lost and replaced more slowly with each decade *(the mechanism and the word "presbyosmia" are not taken from the course material)* | **true** |\n| **Hyperosmia in schizophrenia** | the deck defines hyperosmia as *"enhanced smell sensitivity"*, and lists *"**Psychiatric conditions**: for example, depression, hysteric conversion reactions, and **schizophrenia**"* among smell disorders — it prints the association, though under its anosmia heading. **Grade Gain prints the psychiatric link to enhanced smell explicitly and is preferred here to outside knowledge:** `entqb-nose2-72` gives a young man with *"increased sensitivity to smells"* and hyperacusis and keys a **psychiatric** cause | **true** |\n| **All of the above** | **keyed** | |\n\n**The three statements are chosen to walk down the pathway, and that is the reason to keep them together.** *Presbyosmia* is attrition at the **receptor sheet** — fewer working cells, so the threshold rises. *Hyperosmia* is a **central gain** problem — the receptors are normal and the brain amplifies. *Agnosia* is **interpretation** — detection is intact, recognition fails. Only the first is a nose problem at all.\n\n**⚠️ Transcription note: the option prints *"szhizophrenia"*.** It is kept exactly as the bank printed it; the correct spelling is **schizophrenia**. This bank’s Nose section has several such typos and the sweep records them rather than silently repairing them.\n\n**Bottom line: not every smell disorder is a loss, and not every loss is in the nose.** Cross-references for the vocabulary: `entqb-nose2-74` and `entqb-nose2-81` (phantosmia), `entep-nose-32` (parosmia), `entqb-nose2-76` (hyposmia as the commonest type), and `enthd-nose-312` (anosmia in a patient whose nose stinks).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Olfactory agnosia is a cortical failure of odour recognition seen after stroke, presbyosmia is the normal age-related decline in smell, and hyperosmia — enhanced smell sensitivity — occurs in psychiatric illness including schizophrenia; all three statements are true.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.90 (Nose ch.3, smell disorders). The osme vocabulary table including *"HYPEROSMIA: enhanced smell sensitivity"*, the olfactory pathway to the primary olfactory cortex, *"Age 1"* on the frequency table and *"Psychiatric conditions: for example, depression, hysteric conversion reactions, and schizophrenia"* are transcribed from `L25.2) smell.pdf`. The psychiatric cause of enhanced smell sensitivity is Grade Gain’s printed key at `entqb-nose2-72`. **The terms *olfactory agnosia* and *presbyosmia* are printed by no cached ENT deck; each is supplied and tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. ⚠️ Source typo transcribed as printed — the option reads *"szhizophrenia"* for **schizophrenia**; noted once in the explanation and not silently corrected.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-312', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'A **22-year-old female** presents with **nasal mucosal atrophy, crustation and a very bad nasal odour perceived by her parents and the physician but NOT by her**. What is the **type of smell disorder** in this patient?',
  options:['Cacosmia','Phantosmia','Anosmia','Parosmia'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Anosmia — she is the only person in the room who cannot smell her own nose, and that is the whole diagnosis.**\n\n**The disease first.** `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` describes primary atrophic rhinitis as a *"chronic nasal disease characterized by **progressive atrophy of nasal mucosa and the underlying bone of the turbinates** with formation of scanty viscid secretions that rapidly dries to form **crusts that emit a characteristic foul odor (ozaena)**. There is associated **abnormal patency of nasal passages**."* Its symptoms include *"nasal obstruction, headache, epistaxis, **bad odor from the nose**, chocking"*, and its signs *"**fetor oris**, green, yellow or black crusts, ulcerated bleeding mucosa, atrophy of inferior & middle turbinates"*. The vignette prints the atrophy, the crusting and the smell — this is atrophic rhinitis, and the young adult female sex fits the deck’s *"endocrinal factor; estrogenic deficiency"* aetiology.\n\n**Now the smell question, which turns entirely on the word NOT.** `L25.2) smell.pdf` defines the four options:\n\n| Term | The deck’s own definition | Does it fit? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cacosmia** | not defined by name in the cached decks, but `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf` uses it as *"**Cacosmia (perception of foul odor)**"*, and all three corpus printings agree it is a foul odour **perceived by the patient** — *"bad odour felt by the patient"* (`entep-nose-55`), *"a false sense of an offensive odor (bad odor felt by the patient)"* (`entep-mfe5-17`), *"perception of foul or unpleasant odors by the patient"* (`entqb-nose2-75`) | **no — she perceives nothing.** This is the designed trap: the odour is real and foul, but *cacosmia* describes who smells it, and it is not her |\n| **Phantosmia** | *"smell perception with **no external stimulus**"* | **no**, twice over: there **is** an external stimulus (the crusts), and she has no perception at all |\n| **Anosmia** | *"**a complete loss of smell**"* | **yes — keyed.** The atrophy has destroyed the olfactory epithelium along with the rest of the mucosa |\n| **Parosmia** | *"**distortion** of perception of external stimulus"* | **no** — distortion requires perception to distort |\n\n**Why atrophic rhinitis in particular takes smell away.** The atrophy is not selective: the same process that thins the respiratory mucosa and resorbs turbinate bone destroys the olfactory epithelium high in the cavity, and the crusts that remain sit over what is left. The disorder is by mechanism **conductive and sensorineural at once** — the deck’s conductive definition covers *"rheological properties of mucus"*, which viscid crusting secretions certainly alter, while the loss of the epithelium is sensorineural. Whichever way it is classified, the result is complete.\n\n**⚠️ The corpus prints both halves of this item separately and keys them the same way:** `entqb-nose3-102` — the smell disorder most likely in atrophic rhinitis → **anosmia**; `entqb-nose3-145` — a woman with *"recurrent nasal crusting and anosmia"* whose *"colleagues complain of a fetid odor"*, with *"thick crusts in a roomy nasal cavity"* → **atrophic rhinitis**. This bank has simply merged the two into one question.\n\n**Bottom line: in ozaena the patient is spared the smell that everyone around them endures — the anosmia is part of the disease, not a separate complaint.** Treatment is aimed at the crusts, not the smell: *"irrigations, humidification to provide moisture, experimental surgical procedures aiming at narrowing the nasal cavity with submucosal implants"* (`L29)`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The patient with atrophic rhinitis (ozaena) has anosmia — the foul odour of her crusts is perceived by everyone except her; cacosmia by contrast is a foul odour perceived by the patient.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.91 (Nose ch.3, smell disorders). Straddles `ent-rhin`; filed to `ent-epistaxis` because the stem asks for the **type of smell disorder**. The definition, aetiology, symptoms, signs and treatment of atrophic rhinitis are transcribed from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the anosmia/parosmia/phantosmia definitions from `L25.2) smell.pdf`; *"Cacosmia (perception of foul odor)"* from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`. **Cacosmia is defined by no cached ENT deck as a full definition — the three corpus printings `entep-nose-55`, `entep-mfe5-17` and `entqb-nose2-75` are used in preference to outside knowledge, and all three agree the odour is perceived BY THE PATIENT. Both halves of this vignette are keyed identically by the corpus at `entqb-nose3-102` and `entqb-nose3-145`.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-313', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-nasalobs',
  stem:'A **4-year-old child** presents with **right-sided nosebleed**. His parents report that he gets a **purulent offensive discharge from the same side**. What is the most likely cause?',
  options:['Septal deviation with right maxillary sinusitis','Antrochoanal polyp','Angiofibroma','Impacted foreign body'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**An impacted foreign body — one nostril, bleeding and stinking, in a four-year-old is the single most stereotyped picture in paediatric rhinology.**\n\nThe vignette prints three discriminators and the lecture answers all three. **Unilateral and foetid:** `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf` marks as **true** the statement *"**Foreign bodies in the nose is the most common cause of unilateral nasal discharge with bad odor in children**"*, and `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` heads its foetid-discharge list with *"1. **Foreign bodies in the nose**"*. **Bleeding:** `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` names the two paediatric causes together — *"Children — **foreign body**, nose picking"* — and lists *"Foreign Bodies"* among the local aetiological factors. **Age four:** `L31)` puts *"3. Foreign bodies"* on its list of causes of nasal obstruction in children.\n\n**The mechanism ties bleeding and discharge to one object.** An impacted body obstructs the side it sits in; secretions stagnate behind it and putrefy, giving the offensive purulent discharge, while the object itself erodes and granulates the mucosa against it — `L26) nasal masses.pdf` files *"FB"* and *"**FB granuloma**"* among **unilateral** nasal masses — and granulation tissue bleeds on the slightest contact. **One lesion, both symptoms, one side.**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| **Septal deviation with right maxillary sinusitis** | the most tempting distractor because it explains both symptoms — but it needs two diagnoses where one will do, and neither is a four-year-old’s. `L31)` files rhinosinusitis under **mucopurulent** discharge, not foetid, and reserves foetid discharge for foreign body, odontogenic sinusitis, neoplasm and fungus. Septal deviation in `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf` is a disease of trauma and growth, presenting with obstruction, not with offensive unilateral pus in a preschooler |\n| **Antrochoanal polyp** | genuinely unilateral — `L26)` lists **ACP** among unilateral masses — but it presents with **obstruction and mucoid discharge**, not putrid pus with bleeding. The corpus’s ACP vignette is a *"9-year-old child… unilateral nasal obstruction together with loud snores and rhinorrhea"* with a *"smooth surfaced mass in the posterior nasal cavity"* (`entep-enr-30`) |\n| **Angiofibroma** | the wrong patient by a decade and the wrong symptom set. `L26)` describes a *"lobulated mass… mainly supplied from ECA (IMAX)"* presenting with unilateral obstruction and **epistaxis**, in adolescent males; the corpus keys it on that demographic repeatedly (`entqb-nose2-59`, `entqb-nose7-228`). It bleeds profusely and does not suppurate |\n| **Impacted foreign body** | **keyed** | \n\n**⚠️ Grade Gain prints the bleeding half of this question by itself and keys it the same way:** `entqb-nose2-84`, *"in the case of unilateral epistaxis in a child, which of the following should be suspected?"* → **foreign body**. The corpus prints the discharge half at `entep-nose-18`, also keyed foreign body.\n\n**Bottom line: in a small child, unilateral is the word that matters — a bilateral runny nose is a cold, a unilateral smelly one is an object.** The examination is anterior rhinoscopy with good light and suction, and the treatment is removal; `enthd-nose-308` is the same lesion presenting with discharge alone.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A unilateral nosebleed with offensive purulent discharge on the same side in a preschool child is an impacted nasal foreign body — obstruction causes the putrid discharge and the surrounding granulation causes the bleeding.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.91 (Nose ch.3). Straddles `ent-epistaxis`; filed to `ent-nasalobs` to match the corpus’s filing of the same lesion at `entep-nose-18` and `entqb-nose2-84`. *"Foreign bodies in the nose is the most common cause of unilateral nasal discharge with bad odor in children"* is from `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`; the foetid- and mucopurulent-discharge lists and the paediatric obstruction list from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`; *"Children — foreign body, nose picking"* and *"Foreign Bodies"* among local factors from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`; *"FB granuloma"*, ACP and the angiofibroma description from `L26) nasal masses.pdf`; septal deviation from `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf`. The ACP presentation used to exclude option B is the corpus’s printed vignette at `entep-enr-30`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-314', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'Which of the following treatment methods is **NOT used** in treatment of **primary epistaxis in children**?',
  options:['Anterior nasal packing','Posterior nasal packing','Nose pinching','Nasal decongestant drops'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Posterior nasal packing — children do not bleed posteriorly, and a posterior pack is far too punishing a procedure for a bleed that a pinch will stop.**\n\n**Why the site rules the treatment.** `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` prints a bimodal age curve and assigns each peak its own compartment: *"The **first peak is during childhood** usually from the **Little’s area**"*, while *"the second peak is in the fifth or sixth decades and is frequently associated with hypertension"* — and posterior bleeding *"comes most commonly from the SPA… a common site of epistaxis in **elderly hypertensive** patients"*. Little’s area is anterior and septal, so every measure that reaches it is anterior.\n\n| Option | Why it is used in a child | Deck’s words |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Nose pinching** | first aid, and the deck calls it the method of choice | *"Direct digital pressure to the lower soft cartilaginous part of the nose by pinching for approximately **10 minutes** is the **method of choice**"*, patient *"sitting and leaning forward, breathing through the mouth"* |\n| **Nasal decongestant drops** | vasoconstrict the anterior septal mucosa and often stop it outright; also used to prepare the field | *"Cotton soaked with **decongestant**"* in the anterior sequence; `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` limits topical drops to *"not more than 5 days to avoid… rhinitis medicamentosa"* |\n| **Anterior nasal packing** | the correct pack for an anterior bleed, used when cautery is unavailable or has failed | *"Anterior Nasal Pack… stops bleeding by pressure and creation of mucosal oedema"* |\n| **Posterior nasal packing** | **keyed as the exception** | see below |\n\n**What a posterior pack costs, in the deck’s own list:** *"Uncomfortable. **Hospitalization.** ET dysfunction and hemotympanum. **Hypoxia and cerebral ischemia. Myocardial infarction.** Toxic shock syndrome. **Failure rate = 20–50 %.**"* Its prerequisites are an admission order — *"continuous cardiopulmonary monitoring, antibiotics, oxygen supplementation… mild sedation/analgesia, IVF"*, with ICU for the frail. Applying that to a child whose bleeding point is a visible spot on the anterior septum is the wrong operation in the wrong compartment.\n\n**⚠️ Grade Gain prints this question almost word for word and keys it identically:** `entqb-nose2-69` — *"primary epistaxis in children may be treated by all of the following except"*, offering compression of Little’s area, decongestant nasal drops, chemical cautery and posterior nasal packing → **posterior nasal packing**. Note that Grade Gain’s menu includes **chemical cautery**, which is also legitimate in a child (`enthd-nose-290`), and this bank has swapped it for anterior packing. **Both banks isolate the posterior pack as the outlier.**\n\n**Bottom line: *primary* means idiopathic and anterior — the corpus keys idiopathic as the commonest cause of childhood epistaxis (`entep-nose-61`) and nose picking as the commonest mechanism (`entqb-nose2-58`) — and anterior disease is treated anteriorly.** A child who genuinely needs a posterior pack has a different diagnosis, and it is usually a tumour or a coagulopathy (`enthd-nose-298`, `enthd-nose-299`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Primary epistaxis in children arises from Little’s area and is treated anteriorly — pinching, decongestant drops, cautery or an anterior pack; posterior packing belongs to elderly posterior bleeds and carries hospitalization, cardiorespiratory risk and a 20–50 % failure rate.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.91 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). The bimodal age curve, Little’s area as the childhood site, the elderly hypertensive posterior bleed, the nose-pinching first-aid slide, *"cotton soaked with decongestant"*, the anterior-pack mechanism and the posterior-pack complication list and prerequisites are transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`; the five-day limit on topical decongestants from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`. **Grade Gain prints this question with the same key at `entqb-nose2-69`; the corpus keys idiopathic and nose picking as the commonest childhood causes at `entep-nose-61` and `entqb-nose2-58`.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-315', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-epistaxis',
  stem:'A **35-year-old male** presents with nosebleed. First aid failed, **anterior nasal packing failed, and combined anterior and posterior packing also failed to stop the epistaxis**. Which of the following would be the appropriate **next step**?',
  options:['Clipping / ligation of internal maxillary artery','Cautery','Blood coagulants','Any of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Ligation of the internal maxillary artery — the deck’s posterior-epistaxis ladder has exactly one rung above the combined pack, and this is it.**\n\n`L25.1) epistaxis.pdf` prints the ladder in order:\n\n> *"**Posterior epistaxis - management**: Deep ant. Pack → **Ant-Post Pack** → **IMAX ligation / Embolization** · Ethmoid ligation · ECA ligation."*\n\nAnd it states when to climb it: among the *"indications for surgery/embolization"* is *"**Continued bleeding despite nasal packing**"* (`enthd-nose-292`). This patient has now failed first aid, an anterior pack and a combined anterior–posterior pack. He is the indication, verbatim.\n\n**Why the maxillary artery specifically.** The bleeding is posterior by definition — *"so far posterior that the bleeding site can not be identified by anterior inspection or controlled by anterior pack"* — and posterior bleeding *"comes most commonly from the **SPA** or one of its branches"*. The **sphenopalatine artery is a terminal branch of the maxillary artery**, which the deck traces as *"SPA → a terminal branch of the maxillary artery → a branch of the ECA"*, with the third part of the maxillary in the pterygopalatine fossa giving *"Vidian, palatovaginal, **SPA**, post. lat. nasal, descending palatine"*. Ligating or clipping the maxillary artery therefore closes the SPA and the greater palatine at once — two of Kiesselbach’s four arteries and the whole posterior supply.\n\n| Option | Why it is not the next step |\n|---|---|\n| **IMAX clipping / ligation** | **keyed** — the rung above the combined pack on the deck’s own ladder |\n| **Cautery** | needs a **visible** bleeding point. This bleed is posterior precisely because the site *"can not be identified by anterior inspection"*; cautery has already been bypassed twice. The endoscopic equivalent — *"cautery of bleeding area **under visualization**"* and *"SPA coagulation at SPF"* — is the same treatment as ligation by another route, but blind cautery of an unseen posterior vessel is not an option |\n| **Blood coagulants** | treat a coagulopathy; nothing in the vignette suggests one, and the deck’s systemic factors are investigated by *"CBC, coagulation profile"* before anything is given. A haemostatic drug will not close an arterial bleed that mechanical pressure has failed to control |\n| **Any of the above** | two of the three are inapplicable here |\n\n**The modern qualification, printed by the deck itself.** Its 2023 slide splits the options into *"Open surgery — IMAX ligation, ethmoid ligation"* and *"Endoscopic — SPA coagulation, ethmoid coagulation"*, and marks *"External carotid ligation — **Obsolete**"*. Its outcome table explains the drift: packing 68 % success, 35 % complications, 4.2 days in hospital; transantral ligation or percutaneous embolization 88 % and 27.5 %; endoscopic *"> 90 %"* with *"No major"* complications and *"< one day"* — hence *"SUGGESTED CHANGE: **initial definitive endoscopic control** +/- temporary packing"*. **Endoscopic SPA coagulation is where practice has moved, and it is the same artery**; the corpus keys it directly at `entep-nose-68` and keys **sphenopalatine ligation** for recurrent posterior epistaxis at `entqb-nose2-67`.\n\n**Bottom line: when packing has failed, go to the artery that feeds the bleed — the maxillary, through its sphenopalatine branch — and not to the external carotid, which is obsolete, or to a drug, which treats a problem this patient does not have.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'When combined anterior and posterior packing fails, the next step is ligation or clipping of the internal maxillary artery — or its modern endoscopic equivalent, sphenopalatine artery coagulation — since posterior epistaxis arises from the sphenopalatine, a terminal maxillary branch.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.91 (Nose ch.3, epistaxis). The posterior-management ladder, the indications for surgery, the definition of posterior epistaxis, the SPA as its usual source, the maxillary artery’s third-part branches, the 2023 open-versus-endoscopic slide with *"External carotid ligation — Obsolete"*, the outcome table and *"initial definitive endoscopic control"* are all transcribed from `L25.1) epistaxis.pdf`. Endoscopic sphenopalatine coagulation and sphenopalatine ligation as the corpus’s printed keys for the same situation are `entep-nose-68` and `entqb-nose2-67`.' }
